NAME
    forkdemo - what fork(2) gives a child, and what it does not

SYNOPSIS
    forkdemo

DESCRIPTION
    Forks once and reports what the two processes can see of each
    other. The child is a second process in its own address space:
    it runs while the parent runs, has its own pid, and resumes at
    the instruction after fork() with a return value of 0. Memory is
    not shared - the child writes to a variable in .data and the
    parent never sees it. The filesystem, the console and every other
    process are shared, and the parent reaps the child with
    waitpid().

    Useful as a smoke test for the process table and for fork()
    itself: every line printed is a claim about what the kernel
    guarantees.

EXIT STATUS
    0 if waitpid() returned the child's pid and its exit status 7,
    1 if fork() failed or the child was not reaped as expected.

SEE ALSO
    ttytest(1), man(1)
